+// OpenStacks (issue #6): cross-context ordering.
+// Every guest GL context is a separate host context, and a colour buffer is written in one
+// and read in another:
+// - an app renders its frame, SurfaceFlinger samples it;
+// - SurfaceFlinger composites, the post thread presents it.
+// The writers put a fence on the buffer's EGLImage (setSync at blit, upload and post), but
+// upstream only waits on it when reading pixels back and posting. The guest's own native
+// fences normally order the rest; the BlueStacks guest stack does not use them and relies
+// on implicit sync. Mesa happens to provide that; NVIDIA's driver runs contexts
+// independently. There, SurfaceFlinger sampled half-drawn app frames (sprites missing
+// for a frame, elements jumping back and forth). So every bind waits on the buffer's
+// last write fence first: a GPU-side glWaitSync, no CPU stall.
 bool ColorBufferGl::bindToTexture() {
     if (!m_eglImage) {
         return false;
@@ -1076,6 +1097,8 @@ bool ColorBufferGl::bindToTexture() {
